# Releasing

Based on [thoughtbot's release template](https://github.com/thoughtbot/templates).

## 1. Update version files accordingly

Bump every file that carries the version, including lockfiles, so they all match. Include a pre-release suffix if appropriate, e.g. `2.0.0-preview.1`.

## 2. Update `CHANGELOG.md` to reflect the changes since last release

`CHANGELOG.md` is for developers: add a `## VERSION — DATE` section with the technical detail — causes, affected code, PR or commit references, known gaps. [GitHub automatically generated release notes] can serve as a starting point.

## 3. Commit changes

There shouldn't be code changes in this commit.

## 4. Tag the release: `git tag -a vVERSION -m "vVERSION"`

## 5. Push changes

Push the branch, then the tag: `git push origin BRANCH && git push origin vVERSION`

## 6. Build and publish

Describe how the tag produces release artifacts (CI workflow, build commands) and how many to expect.

## 7. Write the GitHub release notes

Release notes are for end users, not developers — do not paste the changelog. Keep them short, one line per item, no internals:

- **New** — what users can now do
- **Fixed** — what used to go wrong, in the user's terms
- **Removed** — what is gone
- **Good to know** — known limits, upgrade steps, unsigned-installer warnings
- **Downloads** — which platforms have installers

Skip empty sections and end with a link to `CHANGELOG.md` for details. GitHub renders every newline in a release body as a line break, so keep each paragraph and list item on one line:

```sh
gh release edit vVERSION --notes-file NOTES.md
```

## 8. Announce the new release

Make sure to say "thank you" to the contributors who helped shape this version!

[GitHub automatically generated release notes]: https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/releasing-projects-on-github/automatically-generated-release-notes#about-automatically-generated-release-notes
